---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navbar from '../components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import LegalPage from '../components/LegalPage.astro';

/**
 * Terms for the site, deliberately not for the work.
 *
 * The most common failure in an agency's terms page is quietly trying to
 * govern client engagements from a page nobody signed. A prospect reading
 * a website has not agreed to payment terms or an IP assignment, and
 * pretending otherwise makes the real contract harder to enforce rather
 * than easier. These cover the site; the engagement letter covers the
 * work, and the page says so.
 *
 * TODO(airaf): governing law is stated as Pakistan, which follows from
 * where the business sits. Confirm it matches the engagement letters —
 * the two disagreeing is worse than either choice.
 */
const updated = '2026-09-05';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Terms of Use — Xtenzium"
  description="The terms covering use of the Xtenzium website: what the content is, what the estimator is and is not, and where the site ends and a client agreement begins."
  path="/terms"
>
  <Navbar />

  <main id="main">
    <LegalPage
      eyebrow="Terms"
      title="The terms for this site."
      summary="These cover the website and nothing else. Work we do for you is governed by a written agreement you actually sign — not by a page you scrolled past."
      updated={updated}
    >
      <h2>What these cover</h2>
      <p>
        This page covers your use of xtenzium.com. It is not a contract for
        services, and reading a website is not entering one. If we work
        together, that engagement is governed by a separate written agreement —
        a scope, a price and terms, signed by both of us. Where the two ever
        disagree, the signed agreement wins.
      </p>

      <h2>The estimator is an estimate</h2>
      <p>
        The project estimator returns a range from four questions. It is a
        starting point for a conversation, deliberately wide, and it is
        explicitly <strong>not a quote and not an offer</strong>. Nothing it
        shows binds either of us. A real number comes after a scoping call,
        in writing, once we understand what is actually being built.
      </p>

      <h2>What is on the site</h2>
      <p>
        We try to keep everything here accurate, and case studies describe real
        projects. Even so, the site is provided as it is: we do not warrant that
        it is complete, current, or uninterrupted, and technical detail written
        about one project is not advice for yours.
      </p>
      <p>
        Figures are labelled with where they come from. A number described as
        measured was taken after launch; a number described as observed is true
        by inspecting the thing itself; an estimate is labelled an estimate. We
        would rather publish a smaller number we can defend than a larger one we
        cannot.
      </p>

      <h2>Ownership</h2>
      <p>
        The design, code, written content and generated artwork on this site
        belong to Xtenzium. You are welcome to read it, link to it, quote it
        with attribution, and share it. You may not republish it as your own or
        pass it off as somebody else's work.
      </p>
      <p>
        Client names, product names and technology marks used across the site
        belong to their respective owners and appear here to describe work and
        tools, not to imply endorsement. Where work was delivered under another
        studio's banner, the site says so and names the contribution rather than
        implying we owned the project.
      </p>

      <h2>Using the site</h2>
      <p>Please do not:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>submit the forms automatically, at volume, or with details that are not yours;</li>
        <li>attempt to access data, accounts or systems you are not entitled to;</li>
        <li>probe, scan or load-test the site without asking us first — ask, and we will usually say yes and tell you when;</li>
        <li>use anything here to build something unlawful.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The forms carry basic anti-abuse measures. If they turn a genuine
        enquiry away, email us instead — that is a bug on our side and we would
        like to know.
      </p>

      <h2>Links out</h2>
      <p>
        The site links to live client work and to source repositories. Those are
        other people's sites running under their own terms and privacy
        practices, and we are not responsible for what happens once you leave.
      </p>

      <h2>Liability</h2>
      <p>
        To the extent the law allows, Xtenzium is not liable for loss arising
        from use of this site or reliance on its content. Nothing here limits
        liability that cannot lawfully be limited, and nothing here affects the
        terms of a signed engagement.
      </p>

      <h2>Law</h2>
      <p>
        These terms are governed by the laws of Pakistan, and disputes about
        this site fall to the courts of Karachi. A signed engagement may specify
        something different, and where it does, it governs that work.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        We may update these terms. The date at the top changes when we do, and
        continuing to use the site after that means the updated version applies.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about any of this go to{' '}
        <a href="mailto:contact@xtenzium.com">contact@xtenzium.com</a>.
      </p>
    </LegalPage>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
